    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3215c48d-3351-57bf-9ea3-261b833ecac9",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3 x^{2} - 1}{y^{2} + 3}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3 x^{2} - 1}{y^{2} + 3} \\\\ (y^{2} + 3)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 x^{2} - 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (y^{2} + 3)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3 x^{2} - 1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (y^{2} + 3)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int (3 x^{2} - 1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (y^{2} + 3)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (3 x^{2} - 1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y^2\\,\\text{d}y+\\int 3\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 3x^2\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{y^3}{3}+3y & =\\frac{3x^3}{3}-1x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^{3}}{3} + 3 y=x^{3} - x+C.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
