    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ad5ae15e-bae7-5cbe-9a05-a2959a8a0793",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3 x^{2} - 2}{y^{2} + 1}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3 x^{2} - 2}{y^{2} + 1} \\\\ (y^{2} + 1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 x^{2} - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (y^{2} + 1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3 x^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (y^{2} + 1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int (3 x^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (y^{2} + 1)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (3 x^{2} - 2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y^2\\,\\text{d}y+\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 3x^2\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{y^3}{3}+1y & =\\frac{3x^3}{3}-2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^{3}}{3} + y=x^{3} - 2 x+C.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
